                            {
                                "example_id": "8c57c06d-3d0f-4665-83bf-eea76c28bd85",
                                "sense_id": "27b602d3-b6b2-3d7b-a387-6cf2698f8b2c",
                                "text": "The company of these excellent animals in my childhood gave a kind of cynomorphic twist to my philosophy and an enjoyment of animal society..."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5d900f9d-a603-41c6-b201-f5c4e2637e7d",
                                "sense_id": "27b602d3-b6b2-3d7b-a387-6cf2698f8b2c",
                                "text": "It was especially the unburied dead whose souls were considered to be cynomorphic in the ancient world: Lilja, Dogs, p. 35."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2e3eb2ca-b606-4487-805c-c2640a6baa8d",
                                "sense_id": "27b602d3-b6b2-3d7b-a387-6cf2698f8b2c",
                                "text": "What do dogs want? They want each other. Human beings are merely a cynomorphic substitute, as we all know."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a4f1e2d2-8346-48af-aa0e-bb95933213ab",
                                "sense_id": "27b602d3-b6b2-3d7b-a387-6cf2698f8b2c",
                                "text": "2009?, Beatrice Chandler Gesell, The Normal Child and Primary Education\nA dog cannot help but be cynomorphic in his mentality and attitude."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
